 work in a  research environment  you probably had minimal opportunities  to conduct research from beginning to  the end and even know about all of the  different stages that go into that kind  of research  and a huge challenge for that is access  to  high quality lab environments  so as an undergraduate student you have  to go through a lot of training and  potentially be in a well-funded  institution to have access to a lot of  that kind of equipment and that's why  bioinformatics is such a critical  opportunity for those who want to  develop those kinds of skills and who  want to stand out  in terms of their capabilities when they  apply for research positions when they  apply for  positions in the industry or even when  they want to continue  their  research and study in a university  so i want to briefly  speak about  the research fellowship program that we  have  that brings the opportunity to work with  big data coming from expert labs  interact with mentors from around the  world that have the background and the  uh kind of track record  and could potentially  give you a recommendation letter at the  end and also the training that we  provide  which will really highlight the  different opportunities in both research  and in industry on how  okay thank you so  um those are the three main components  of this program right and so i'll  briefly kind of show  how these three different components are  used in several different directions  that i think are very promising  in the world of bioinformatics where  really you can see a lot of data coming  to the forefront of biological discovery  and translational research that leads  into biotechnology pharmaceutical  industry and the clinical research  field  so  there are  kind of four of these topics that i will  briefly talk about  precision medicine population health  biotechnology and agriculture and space  and astrobiology  these four have been chosen because  the amount of data the complexity of  data and the reliance on computational  biology skills individuals  infrastructure etc  is growing at an exponential rate  so let's first talk about precision  medicine  what we all know is when we think about  the world medicine is that at the  beginning of that journey in medicine  you  meet a doctor or a nurse  and  uh you as you probably all know when you  go to a doctor you go to a clinic  the first thing that they're going to do  is they're going to try to diagnose the  disease and diagnosing the disease is a  process that involves expertise so a lot  of experience that doctors have dealing  with this specific type of disease it  requires labs where your samples are  being sent to  identify what's going on you know what  exactly is this  and then it also has this  large field of research that constantly  is being updated because people are  finding new meaningful insights about  different types of diseases and and  conditions that we call uh pre-clinical  conditions that lead to disease  so in this field of precision medicine  this whole change from  having  a few indicators that can tell us very  accurately what the disease is that is  changing to  many many more indicators that are much  more precise that's where the word  precision comes from in the term  precision medicine but also because they  are so precise they are specific to  very small groups or even individuals  and that's where the word personalized  medicine comes from  so those two parallel directions of  precision  and personalized medicine  converge in specific chronic diseases  and those could be cancer metabolic  diseases  and others and and they include a  combination of various markers that can  help us understand the risk how  predisposed someone is to have a disease  happen early on  complexity of disease progression so how  likely are they to recover fast or  develop some complications  but also importantly to two very  specific and immediate needs in this  field which is accurate diagnosis and  then 