h in a profound  way  and that's why there's a huge effort in  not only building technology to get us  to space but also developing  methodologies to understand how space  travel will affect health and how we can  adapt to these conditions to be able to  grow food maybe on the space station  maybe  as you probably have seen the first  arabidopsis plant has recently been  grown in moon soil here on earth but it  was on in moon soil and and that is a  significant study that shows that it is  possible to grow food on the moon  theoretically  but also there have been studies about  the microbiome  because there's an issue with  different types of microbes kind of  living in the space station  international space station right so  there's a huge interest and funding  available for those kinds of studies  and in fact many of our interns and  fellows have been able to conduct  research studies using data that nasa is  actually providing free of charge to the  community at large  kind of encouraging students researchers  to be asking questions about what's  happening in space and how can we  understand and better prepare  for the type of space travel that is  envisioned by these space agencies  so  to  start addressing the variety of topics  that we just discussed i think uh dr  raghavendran already provided you with a  clear framework on how to be able to  address some of those questions but i  want to re review again a couple of the  slides that you already shared but  maybe focus on them in a little bit of a  different fashion so the first thing  that he mentioned was  the challenge with selecting a topic we  just saw  uh four major domains a lot of potential  directions and a lot of interesting  questions that could be asked and i  think as you get to know every one of  these domains and you  are able to study the research  publications out there that come out or  have come out in the recent years you  will find that a lot of those papers  they focus on organizing and  